6. UX/UI Design System
The interface should feel like a quiet professional room: calm, private, clear and emotionally safe—not like a hospital
dashboard or game.
Client UX

Link & Heal — Full Product & Technical Blueprint Page 4
• One primary action per screen.
• Progressive disclosure: optional fields appear only when enabled by the therapist.
• No pressure-based gamification, streaks or competitive mechanics.
• Large touch targets, accessible type and simple language.
• Clear task states: Not started / In progress / Completed / Missed.
• Private/shared/clinical content visually separated.
• Generic notifications and safe lock-screen behavior.
• Offline mood entry should feel the same as online entry.
Visual recommendations
Area Direction
Color Soft neutral foundation with restrained blue/teal accent; semantic risk colors only where needed.
Cards Rounded, spacious, low-density; minimal shadows.
Typography Readable system font stack, scalable/dynamic type.
Navigation Simple bottom navigation for clients; sidebar/navigation rail for therapist web.
Charts Simple line/bar charts with readable labels and session markers.
Forms Short steps, autosave drafts, clear completion states.
Accessibility WCAG 2.1 AA target, screen reader labels, contrast, focus order, scalable text.
7. Localization — English Now, Languages Later
Release 1 is English-only. The application should still be built around localization keys rather than hard-coded
English strings.
• Translation files separate from application logic.
• Locale-aware date, time and number formatting.
• Layouts tolerate longer translations.
• Direction-aware layout so RTL can be enabled later.
• Questionnaire content supports language/version metadata where licensing permits.
• User language preference can be introduced without rewriting business logic.
8. Mood Module — Detailed Behavior
• Multiple entries per day.
• Core value: 0–10 mood with expressive symbols.
• Optional emotions: anxiety, sadness, anger, calm, hope.
• Optional therapist-enabled fields: anxiety, sleep hours/quality, energy, irritability, substance craving.
• Optional context tags: work, relationship, family, health and free text.
• Client-selected reminder time.
• Client weekly/monthly graphs; therapist graphs include session-date markers.
• Example alert: drop of 3+ points for 3 consecutive days; threshold configurable per client.
Recommendation: store the alert rule version/configuration used for each client so historical alerts remain
explainable if defaults change.
9. Assessment Engine
• Therapist selects an assessment and schedule.
• Client completes item-by-item with save/resume.
• Scoring is server-side and based on versioned scoring rules.
• Severity classification uses the validated cutoffs for that assessment version.
• Item-level answers remain available to authorized therapists.

Link & Heal — Full Product & Technical Blueprint Page 5
• History shows scores over time.
• RCI is calculated according to the agreed Truax & Jacobson (1991) method and validated by the therapist.
• Custom questionnaires use controlled question/choice templates.
Named measures in the source requirements
PHQ-9, GAD-7, PCL-5, K10, CSI-4/CSI-16, RCADS, SRS/ORS, DAS and selected Gottman tools. Licensing/copyright
must be verified before implementation; protected text is not copied without authorization.
10. Safety / Risk Workflow
Example required flow: client submits PHQ-9 item 9 > 0 → risk rule evaluates immediately → crisis-resource screen →
therapist in-app/email notification → clear notice that therapist may not see it immediately → audit event.
• The app states it is not an emergency service and is not monitored 24/7.
• Risk resources are configurable.
• No clinical detail appears in ordinary push notification text.
• Risk events are auditable.
• The workflow is tested during poor connectivity and notification failure.
11. Couples Privacy — Critical Architecture
Each partner has a completely separate account. A couple profile links the accounts but does not merge their
private data.
Visibility Who can see it?
PRIVATE Author + therapist. Default.
SHARED Both partners + therapist.
CLINICAL_ONLY Therapist only; used for sensitive clinical/safety information.
• Authorization is enforced server-side and preferably at database level as defense in depth.
• Direct object IDs and API calls must not bypass visibility.
• Unauthorized records are omitted rather than merely hidden by the UI.
• Separation or consent withdrawal immediately revokes partner access.
• Quick-exit closes/obscures the app immediately.
• IPV screening is individual and never exposed to the other partner.
• Relationship check-ins are completed separately; therapist can see a comparison/gap view.
12. Homework, Resources & Goals
• Homework library supports Word/PDF and interactive forms.
• Templates: CBT thought record, behavioral activation, SUDS, ACT values, DBT diary card and sleep diary.
• Assignments contain instructions, due date, frequency and reminders.
• Client can complete in app or upload a handwritten-photo.
• States: not started / in progress / completed / missed.
• Therapist can comment on completed homework.
• Completion percentage appears on therapist dashboard.
• Resources include short articles and breathing/grounding audio.
• Goals use Goal Attainment Scaling (−2 to +2).
• Unified progress timeline combines mood, measures, homework, goals and session markers.
• PDF progress report and CSV export are supported subject to permissions/consent.
13. Notifications

Link & Heal — Full Product & Technical Blueprint Page 6
Event Safe text
New homework You have a new task.
Assessment You have a new task to complete.
Therapist comment You have a new update.
Resource You have a new resource.
Reminder You have a reminder.
Risk Do not expose clinical content in push text; use the defined safety flow.
Client controls include reminder time, quiet hours and pause reminders.
14. Recommended Frontend Architecture
The source allows Flutter or React-based development. Recommendation: React Native + TypeScript for
iOS/Android, with a React/Next.js-style web dashboard. This is an implementation recommendation, not a fixed
client requirement.
• Monorepo with shared types, validation schemas, design tokens and API client.
• Feature modules: auth, consent, mood, assessments, homework, goals, progress, resources, notifications, couples,
profile/settings.
• Secure local storage and encrypted offline queue for mood.
• Server-state caching with explicit invalidation after mutations.
• Centralized UI permission helpers, while the server remains authoritative.
• Automated critical-flow UI tests.
UI → feature state/hooks → domain services → typed API client → secure storage/offline queue
15. Recommended Backend Architecture
Recommended: modular backend API, e.g. NestJS/TypeScript or equivalent. Keep business modules separate while
deploying as a controlled application rather than a runtime plugin system.
Module Responsibilities
Identity/Auth Login, sessions, therapist 2FA, device/PIN/biometric coordination.
Consent Documents, versions, signatures, dates, withdrawal.
Clients Profiles and therapist-client relationships.
Mood Entries, trends, thresholds, alert evaluation.
Assessments Library metadata, assignments, responses, scoring, RCI.
Homework Templates, assignments, submissions, comments.
Goals Treatment goals, GAS and progress.
Couples/Family Links, visibility, sharing, separation/revocation.
Resources Articles, audio, assignments.
Notifications Generic templates, schedules, quiet hours, delivery.
Reports PDF/CSV generation.
Audit Immutable access/change events.
Admin Controlled operational configuration.
16. Database — Recommended Model
PostgreSQL is a strong fit because the system is relational, permission-heavy, transaction-sensitive and
audit-oriented.

Link & Heal — Full Product & Technical Blueprint Page 7
Entity Purpose
users / roles Identity and authorization.
therapist_clients Explicit therapist-client relationship.
consents / consent_versions Consent version/signature history.
clients Profile and therapist-controlled configuration.
mood_entries Mood and optional wellbeing/context.
mood_alert_rules / alerts Versioned rules and generated alerts.
assessments / versions Immutable assessment definitions.
assignments / responses / response_items Assessment lifecycle and item-level data.
scoring_rules / results Versioned scoring and severity.
homework_templates / assignments / submissionsHomework lifecycle.
goals / measurements GAS and progress.
couples / family / memberships Relationship grouping.
content_visibility PRIVATE / SHARED / CLINICAL_ONLY.
resources / assignments Resource delivery.
notifications / preferences Safe notifications and quiet hours.
audit_events Immutable audit trail.
files / file_metadata Private file storage metadata.
Recommended defense in depth: API authorization + PostgreSQL Row-Level Security where practical + integration
tests that actively attempt unauthorized access.
