18. Security Architecture
Layer Control
Transport TLS 1.2+ and secure certificates.
At rest AES-256 or equivalent approved encryption.
Authentication Strong passwords; therapist MFA/2FA.
Client unlock PIN/biometric app unlock.

Link & Heal — Full Product & Technical Blueprint Page 8
Layer Control
Authorization Server-side role + relationship + visibility checks.
Database Least privilege, encrypted backups, RLS where appropriate.
Audit Immutable access/change records.
Files Private storage + short-lived authorized URLs.
Secrets Managed secrets; never source-controlled.
Notifications Generic text; no clinical payload/lock-screen detail.
Analytics No external analytics that transmit client data.
AI No external AI processing without explicit consent/data-protection agreement.
Testing Independent penetration test before launch and annually.
Incidents Written breach response plan and required notifications.
The source requirements specify Canadian-only hosting for servers, databases and backups. Examples named in the
document include AWS ca-central-1 and Azure Canada Central.
19. Threat Model
Scenario Defense
Partner accesses private answer API authorization + database policy + direct-ID tests.
Notification exposes diagnosis Generic templates and minimal payloads.
Lost/stolen phone PIN/biometric, session timeout, encrypted local data.
Compromised therapist account 2FA, session controls, audit logging.
Unauthorized client record access Relationship-based authorization.
Leaked file URL Private storage + short-lived URLs.
Offline device exposure Encrypted local store and minimal cached data.
Unapproved vendor receives data Vendor/data-flow inventory and restrictions.
Developer sees real data Synthetic data only for development/testing.
20. Offline & File Handling
• Mood entry works offline.
• Sensitive offline data is encrypted.
• Pending sync uses unique IDs and idempotent requests.
• Clinical records are never silently overwritten on conflict.
• Private files use object storage, with database metadata only.
• Access URLs are short-lived and authorized.
• Server enforces file type/size limits; malware scanning is recommended.
21. AI Policy
AI is not a required Phase 1 feature in the source requirements. If introduced later, it must not become an
uncontrolled route for client data outside the approved environment.
• Explicit consent before external AI processing.
• Appropriate data-protection agreement.
• No autonomous diagnosis or crisis decisions.
• Therapist review before AI-generated clinical summaries enter a record.
• Auditability of AI-related access and transformations.

Link & Heal — Full Product & Technical Blueprint Page 9
22. Hosting, Environments & Deployment
Environment Purpose
Local Synthetic data only.
Development Feature integration and tests.
Staging Production-like acceptance/security tests; no real client data.
Production Canadian infrastructure; real data only after privacy/security acceptance.
• Infrastructure as code is recommended.
• CI: lint, type checks, unit/integration tests and security checks.
• Separate secrets per environment.
• Version-controlled database migrations.
• Encrypted Canadian backups.
• Operational logs contain no clinical content.
23. QA & Acceptance
Test layer Examples
Unit Mood alerts, scoring, RCI, GAS, permission functions.
Integration API authorization, DB policies, consent and notification flows.
E2E Client assessment → score → therapist result.
Security Partner isolation, IDOR/direct links, role escalation.
Accessibility Screen reader, dynamic type, contrast, web keyboard/focus.
Offline Create mood offline → reconnect → exactly-once sync.
Performance Dashboard/pre-session summary under realistic volume.
Penetration Independent external test before launch and annually.
Critical acceptance tests from the requirements
• PHQ-9 item 9 = 1 reaches therapist within the defined target and crisis screen appears.
• Partner A cannot access Partner B private answers through UI, direct links or API.
• Assessment scoring matches manual scoring in 20 therapist-defined cases.
• No clinical content in notifications or lock-screen previews.
• Every client-file access appears in audit log.
• Future Arabic interface renders correctly RTL, including charts/tables.
24. Data Lifecycle & Privacy Governance
• Collect only what an enabled feature or clinical workflow needs.
• Store consent version/date and withdrawal state.
• Retention aligns with applicable professional record requirements and legal advice.
• Secure final deletion after the approved retention period.
• Exports are permission-checked and auditable.
• Privacy impact assessment and legal review before launch.
• Breach-response plan includes applicable Alberta privacy notifications.
25. Development Roadmap

Link & Heal — Full Product & Technical Blueprint Page 10
Stage Scope Exit criteria
0 — Foundation Architecture, design system, auth, environments, consent, security baseline.Threat model + architecture review + secure test environmen
1 — MVP Mood, PHQ-9/GAD-7, safety, homework, basic dashboard, security. Acceptance tests + 5–10 client pilot readiness.
2 — Clinical Expansion Couples privacy, more measures, RCI, GAS, reports, pre-session summary. Therapist validates calculations/workflows.
3 — Advanced Family, form builder, optional messaging/audio, future integrations. Feature-specific privacy/security review.
Launch Pen test, legal/privacy review, production, handoff. No critical security findings + documented acceptance.
26. Ownership & Handoff
• Client owns IP and source code.
• Repository, cloud, Apple, Google Play and domain accounts remain under client control.
• Developer uses test data only.
• Milestone payments tied to acceptance criteria.
• Warranty and maintenance/security updates documented separately.
• Full technical documentation and handoff required.
• Client retains access if the development relationship ends.
27. Open Decisions Before Development
• Final framework: Flutter vs React Native/React-based.
• Exact Canadian cloud provider/region and backup design.
• Licensed assessment list and approved text.
• Final crisis-resource wording and safety protocol.
• Retention/deletion schedule after privacy/legal review.
• Whether messaging is in the initial release.
• Final brand, colors, typography and component system.
• Therapist alert/escalation workflow.
• Penetration testing provider/scope.
• Future practice-management integration target.
28. Recommended High-Level Architecture
Client iOS/Android
↓
Typed API Client + Secure Offline Queue
↓
Application API
├─ Identity & Consent
├─ Relationship Authorization
├─ Mood & Alerts
├─ Assessment & Scoring
├─ Homework / Goals / Resources
├─ Couples / Family Visibility
├─ Notifications
├─ Reports / Export
└─ Immutable Audit
↓
PostgreSQL + Private Object Storage
↓
Canadian Infrastructure + Encrypted Backups
Therapist Web Dashboard uses the same API and authorization model; it never bypasses the security layer to query the database directly.
Final principle: the client experience should feel simple; the underlying architecture carries the
complexity required for privacy, clinical integrity, relationship separation, auditability and safe
data handling.

